@antirez Use case I keep hitting:

A local classifier doing financial categorization, ~100 inferences per run. The system prompt is identical on every call; the user prompt is small and varies per call. With the current flags I can't get a stable cache hit across the run: `--kv-cache-boundary-trim-tokens` trims a fixed-length tail off the cold store, but the system/user boundary is structural, not length-based. So depending on how long each user message happens to be, the cut lands either inside the system prompt or past it (the saved prefix now contains user tokens that won't match next time).

`--kv-cache-anchor-user` cuts the cold store at the first <|User|> token instead of at a length-aligned boundary, so the saved prefix is exactly the system tokens. Every subsequent request hits at the system-prompt boundary regardless of user-message length. Off by default. The existing trim/align path is unchanged and still handles raw /v1/completions, where there's no <|User|> token to anchor on.

