Follow-up from the focused repro/debug run.

I instrumented the first generated token after `/switch` to dump top logits before sampling. In failing cases the logits were invalid after `ds4_session_sync()`:

```text
first-token-top rank=0 id=-1 logit=-1e+30 logprob=-1e+30 text=
...
first-token-top rank=15 id=-1 logit=-1e+30 logprob=-1e+30 text=
first-token-sampled id=0 text=<｜begin▁of▁sentence｜>
```

Passing cases had sane logits, e.g.:

```text
first-token-top rank=0 id=13455 logit=37.7091 logprob=-0.0057538 text=ACT
first-token-sampled id=13455 text=ACT
```

So the failure does not look like ordinary prompt confusion or random bad sampling. After restoring a saved session with `/switch`, the next sync can leave generation with unusable logits even though cache reuse/session state otherwise looks valid.

I tested a local patch with two guardrails:

1. after `ds4_session_sync()`, call `ds4_session_top_logprobs(..., 1)`;
2. if the top candidate is invalid (`nsanity <= 0`, id `< 0`, or non-finite logit), call `ds4_session_invalidate()` and rebuild from the full prompt via `ds4_session_sync()` before sampling;
3. as a secondary guard, suppress generated BOS token id `0` during assistant generation with `ds4_session_argmax_excluding()`.

Results:

```text
baseline corrected matrix: 12 cases, 8 first-answer BOS failures
instrumented/toplog matrix: 12 cases, 9 first-answer BOS failures; invalid top logits confirmed
invalid-logits rebuild only: 12 cases, 0 first-answer failures, 0 first-answer BOS pathologies, 1 second-answer failure
rebuild + generated-BOS guard: 12 cases, 0 first-answer failures, 0 BOS pathologies, 0 second-answer failures
clean final patch validation: 12 cases, 0 first-answer failures, 0 BOS pathologies, 0 second-answer failures
```

In the clean final validation, all 12 cases triggered the invalid-logits rebuild and the generated-BOS guard did not fire. The BOS guard is there because the rebuild-only run still had one mid-generation BOS loop after a valid answer prefix.

Minimal patch shape:

```c
ds4_token_score sanity[1];
int nsanity = ds4_session_top_logprobs(w->session, sanity, 1);
if (nsanity <= 0 || sanity[0].id < 0 || !isfinite(sanity[0].logit)) {
    agent_trace(w, "post-sync logits invalid; rebuilding session from full prompt");
    ds4_session_invalidate(w->session);
    ds4_session_set_progress(w->session, worker_progress_cb, w);
    if (ds4_session_sync(w->session, prompt_for_sync, err, sizeof(err)) != 0) {
        ds4_session_set_progress(w->session, NULL, NULL);
        agent_set_error(w, err);
        return 1;
    }
    ds4_session_set_progress(w->session, NULL, NULL);
    nsanity = ds4_session_top_logprobs(w->session, sanity, 1);
    if (nsanity <= 0 || sanity[0].id < 0 || !isfinite(sanity[0].logit)) {
        agent_set_error(w, "post-sync logits remained invalid after full rebuild");
        return 1;
    }
}
```

And during generation:

```c
int token = ds4_session_sample(w->session, cfg->gen.temperature, 0,
                               cfg->gen.top_p, cfg->gen.min_p, &rng);
if (token == 0) {
    int alt = ds4_session_argmax_excluding(w->session, token);
    agent_trace(w, "suppressed generated BOS token at index=%d alt=%d",
                generated + 1, alt);
    token = alt;
}
if (token == ds4_token_eos(w->engine)) break;
```

The more likely true root cause is still below this guard: restored-session sync can return success but leave logits invalid. The full-prompt rebuild is a pragmatic repair path and a useful diagnostic boundary.

